WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:01.480
- [Mark] All right, Leslie.

00:00:01.480 --> 00:00:02.640
Leslie, where'd you grow up?

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:03.473
Where are you from?

00:00:03.473 --> 00:00:07.548
- Baltimore, Maryland, Charm City.

00:00:07.548 --> 00:00:09.500
- [Mark] Okay (chuckles).

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:10.333
Tell me about your family.

00:00:10.333 --> 00:00:11.880
Had both your parents growing up?

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.820
- No, I never met my father until later on

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:18.610
but in fact about six years
ago, I actually met him.

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:22.290
But my mom's was my primary caretaker.

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:24.940
I have two younger brothers

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:27.403
one of them now deceased
the youngest brother.

00:00:28.362 --> 00:00:31.640
My brother Craig is a, he retired

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:34.710
he was a correctional
officer for the Department

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:38.153
of Public Safety and Correctional
Services in Baltimore.

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:41.080
- [Mark] And tell me about your childhood.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:42.810
You had a good childhood?

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:44.490
- Yeah, I had a pretty good childhood.

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:47.630
I mean, I spent a lot of my
time traveling back and forth.

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:52.013
I actually was raised by my
godmother in Brooklyn, New York.

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:59.100
I would be sent back to my
mom's house to spend time

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:01.220
in the summer there in Baltimore.

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:03.660
- [Mark] Why were you not
with your mom full time?

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:05.970
Why were you not with your mom full time?

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:06.830
- To be honest with you.

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:09.800
I really believe it was
because of her animosity

00:01:11.419 --> 00:01:16.400
and sincere attitude with
my father whom I never met.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:20.363
I bear the burden of
being his child, okay.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:23.070
- [Mark] And then tell me,

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:24.590
what kind of kid were you in high school?

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:26.390
- In high school I was pretty good.

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:27.560
As far as the student,

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:29.990
I was real good with working stuff out.

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:33.210
I did my work and got good grades and all,

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:36.890
but I actually started to excel

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:39.597
in playing basketball in school.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:44.240
From junior high school up to high school

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:47.660
I actually played on
the junior varsity team

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:49.000
and then the varsity team

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.940
at Southern High School
in Baltimore, Maryland

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:53.460
when I was there.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:56.130
Prior to that I played
in the neighborhood,

00:01:56.130 --> 00:02:00.100
they had a league that they
started, they call BNBL

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:03.870
which is the Baltimore
Neighborhood Basketball League,

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:06.260
where the communities will play

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:07.800
against each other in the city.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.720
And then at the end, they will have

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:13.050
a citywide championship
that will actually play

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:15.490
at the place called the
Baltimore Civic Center

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:18.063
which is now called the Baltimore Arena.

00:02:19.416 --> 00:02:23.240
I was able to hone my skills
and become more experienced

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:26.860
in playing ball and was getting write-ups

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:30.890
in the News-American newspaper,
the Baltimore Sun paper,

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:33.483
in regardless to being player of the week.

00:02:35.862 --> 00:02:38.870
I actually started getting recognition

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:43.563
for my ability to play
basketball at school.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:49.500
My coaches and them were
actually treating me

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:53.855
to the point where, "Look
Leslie you have the ability

00:02:53.855 --> 00:02:55.237
"and people are starting
to recognize this,

00:02:55.237 --> 00:02:57.927
"and it's a possibility
you might go forward

00:02:57.927 --> 00:03:02.170
"if you consider playing
ball in college and all."

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:03.293
Which I did.

00:03:06.388 --> 00:03:07.638
- [Mark] Were you getting a scholarship?

00:03:07.638 --> 00:03:08.580
- Yeah, I was looking for scholarships,

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:14.633
North Carolina State, USC,

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:19.077
Maryland naturally,
University of Maryland,

00:03:19.077 --> 00:03:21.520
Lefty Driesell down there in Maryland.

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:23.460
I met with him and talked to him before,

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:26.910
actually before I was
wrongfully convicted and all

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:29.943
in regards to actually
considering plan at Maryland.

00:03:33.127 --> 00:03:37.140
I had opportunities to meet
with coaches and so forth

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:39.490
in New York when I was
living in New York also

00:03:39.490 --> 00:03:40.800
in regards to playing ball.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:44.490
I've also played in the
Rutgers League up there too

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.863
over at Manhattan, up in Harlem.

00:03:48.232 --> 00:03:49.873
- [Mark] And your high school team was?

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:53.300
- My high school team
was extremely strong.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:57.200
We actually went forth
and played against Dunbar

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:01.280
which was a leading high school
in the State of Maryland.

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:04.510
They had Skip Wise, they had Larry Gibson

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:07.830
who played center for Dunbar.

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:10.150
Dunbar's coach was a well-known coach

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:13.450
throughout the Scholastic
Basketball League.

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:14.803
His name was Sugar Cain.

00:04:17.405 --> 00:04:19.710
These are the individuals
that I played basketball with

00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:21.103
during the time that I was,

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:24.200
well, before I was wrongfully convicted.

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:27.600
These were the people who
actually were ball players

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:29.723
in that era of time that I played at.

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:34.310
- [Mark] And then tell me what happened

00:04:34.310 --> 00:04:35.513
to you at 17 years old?

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:37.950
You're still a high school kid, right?

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:39.900
- Yeah I was still a high school kid.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:43.480
I worked in the, what
they call Muhly's Bakery,

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:48.480
which was a bakery that had
been in business for many years.

00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:53.703
It was located near a Cross Street Market,

00:04:54.866 --> 00:04:56.660
and anyone who is familiar with Maryland

00:04:56.660 --> 00:05:00.747
is familiar with the
Inner Harbor, Fells Point,

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:07.340
those areas are areas of
prominence where people resided at.

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:10.850
Well, this bakery that
I worked at was situated

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:12.253
in that community.

00:05:13.186 --> 00:05:16.230
In the evening after I
had basketball practice

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:19.006
I would go to work at the
bakery, would stay there

00:05:19.006 --> 00:05:21.090
and work until later in the evening,

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:23.700
and then leave and go to
my grandfather's house

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:25.590
who lived at one Hanover Street,

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:28.240
which was a couple of blocks
from the market and all.

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:34.020
Just by coincidence I
was at home with my mom,

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:36.956
this one day, I think it was a Saturday,

00:05:36.956 --> 00:05:39.830
and she asked me to go to the
store and get a newspaper,

00:05:39.830 --> 00:05:42.680
something that I been doing for years,

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:44.970
you know, it was a normal thing.

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:48.240
So I went to, walked over to a drug store,

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:51.760
it's called a Westport
Pharmacy on Annapolis Road,

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:55.680
which was the only neighborhood
store in the community.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:58.760
And I went in, I spoke
to doc who was the owner

00:05:58.760 --> 00:05:59.593
of the store.

00:05:59.593 --> 00:06:03.470
I spoke with, Clarence McCleary,
we call Old Man Clarence,

00:06:03.470 --> 00:06:06.650
and Mr. Willie Adams,
Willie, Old Man Willie.

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:08.690
They were full-time delivery drivers

00:06:08.690 --> 00:06:12.090
for the Westport Pharmacy
that was in the neighborhood

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:13.520
that I lived in.

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:15.830
I spoke with them and
then I went up front,

00:06:15.830 --> 00:06:18.713
I made the purchase and
I came out of the store.

00:06:19.783 --> 00:06:20.616
- [Mark] Just a newspaper?

00:06:20.616 --> 00:06:22.660
- Yeah, the newspaper that's
all she wanted was the news

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:24.510
'cause she read the paper and stuff--

00:06:25.475 --> 00:06:27.309
- [Mark] And you're basically
just a kid whose life--

00:06:27.309 --> 00:06:28.142
- I was a kid.

00:06:28.142 --> 00:06:29.030
I was a kid.

00:06:29.030 --> 00:06:29.863
- [Mark] Your life revolved

00:06:29.863 --> 00:06:31.220
around basketball and school.

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:33.530
- My life was about basketball.

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:35.510
I didn't have no girlfriends.

00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:37.760
I wasn't interested in no girlfriend.

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:40.770
The only girlfriend I
had was that basketball,

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:42.540
- [Mark] No gangs, nothing like that?

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:44.870
- No gangs and all that
stuff during that time,

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:47.720
if it was it wasn't in the
community that I lived in.

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:49.540
It was none of that.

00:06:49.540 --> 00:06:50.550
It was none of the things that you see

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:52.000
now that's transpired.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:53.720
It was a wholesome environment,

00:06:53.720 --> 00:06:57.400
kids used to go out in the
street and play marbles

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:01.480
and skillet and red hot butter beans

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:03.503
and all those kinda games and stuff.

00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:07.410
Kids were kids or adults were adults.

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:11.690
Kids knew what they suppose,
was required of them.

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:14.420
When the lights, when it
started getting dark outside

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:16.370
and the street lights start coming on,

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:19.700
you know to be home, you
know those kinds of things.

00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:22.850
And I came out the drug store.

00:07:22.850 --> 00:07:25.810
I remember standing there
waiting to cross the street

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:27.930
coming back towards my home.

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:31.142
And I heard this man say,
"You with the leather coat on

00:07:31.142 --> 00:07:34.290
"go put your hands against the wall."

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:36.350
I don't know, I heard it,

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:38.350
but I wasn't really paying
on mind, because I knew

00:07:38.350 --> 00:07:40.864
they couldn't have been talking to me.

00:07:40.864 --> 00:07:42.383
I didn't know was going on.

00:07:43.433 --> 00:07:46.240
So I looked and it was an
officer, a police officer

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:49.320
with this revolver pulled
out and he was pointing it.

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:51.287
And he said, "You get up against the wall

00:07:51.287 --> 00:07:53.500
"put your hands against the wall."

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:56.267
And I looking, I'm looking, 'cause I know,

00:07:56.267 --> 00:07:58.480
but I'm assuming he's not talking to me

00:07:58.480 --> 00:07:59.510
'cause I ain't done nothing.

00:07:59.510 --> 00:08:00.880
I don't know what's going on.

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:02.337
I pointed myself, and he said,

00:08:02.337 --> 00:08:04.680
"Yeah, you with the beige leather coat,

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:06.140
put your hands against the wall.

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:08.160
I said, "What'd I do?

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:10.350
He said, "Get up against the wall."

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:13.490
And I walked over, put my
hands against the wall,

00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:17.226
and he came up behind me and he said,

00:08:17.226 --> 00:08:18.070
"Put your face against the wall,"

00:08:18.070 --> 00:08:20.860
and put the gun to the back of my head.

00:08:20.860 --> 00:08:24.160
And he said, "You robbed a drug store."

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:26.640
I said, "No, I didn't, I just
come out to the drugstore.

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:29.767
I said, "Doc can tell you,
I didn't rob no drugstore,

00:08:29.767 --> 00:08:32.040
"he know me, he know who I am."

00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:33.777
And the guy was, the officer was like,

00:08:33.777 --> 00:08:35.700
"I don't care, I don't
wanna hear none of that."

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:39.557
Then I heard him say, "Is this the guy?"

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:43.300
And the man, I heard a voice,

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:46.210
only thing, because of him
having a gun against my head,

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:49.700
my face against the wall, I
was afraid to turn my head

00:08:51.414 --> 00:08:53.263
'cause I didn't want this man to shoot me.

00:08:54.917 --> 00:08:56.508
The week to prior to this

00:08:56.508 --> 00:09:00.176
there was an incident that
was, that had been documented,

00:09:00.176 --> 00:09:02.190
and they was talking about
and people were protesting

00:09:02.190 --> 00:09:06.860
because a officer was supposedly
shot at another teenager

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:09.383
in Baltimore in some other community.

00:09:11.343 --> 00:09:15.243
And he said, "You're under
arrest for robbing a drug store.

00:09:18.644 --> 00:09:20.160
"the police wagon's coming to get you."

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:22.250
And I'm like, "Well, I didn't do nothing."

00:09:22.250 --> 00:09:26.050
So I said, "Ask doc,
I didn't do anything."

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:27.220
And he said, "I don't wanna hear it."

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:30.760
He said, "You've got to tell
it to the judge and all that."

00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:32.537
All the while I'm saying to myself,

00:09:32.537 --> 00:09:33.577
"God what is going on?

00:09:33.577 --> 00:09:36.390
"I came here to get a newspaper now what?"

00:09:36.390 --> 00:09:39.100
So it was other people standing out there

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:41.127
that lived in that community,

00:09:41.127 --> 00:09:43.297
and the people was like,
"Leslie, what's going on?

00:09:43.297 --> 00:09:45.190
"Why them police messing
with you and all that?"

00:09:45.190 --> 00:09:46.820
I said, "He said, I
robbed the drug store."

00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:48.300
He said, "Rob the drug store?"

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:50.830
These are people in the
community standing around

00:09:50.830 --> 00:09:53.130
because across the street
from this drugstore

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:55.610
was homes, was houses.

00:09:55.610 --> 00:09:58.440
And these people knew
me from the neighborhood

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:01.830
and they was like,
"Well, what's going on?"

00:10:01.830 --> 00:10:03.170
They tried to talk to the police,

00:10:03.170 --> 00:10:06.493
trying to tell him,
"This kid lives out here.

00:10:07.856 --> 00:10:09.147
"They know him.

00:10:09.147 --> 00:10:13.227
"All you've got to do is ask
the owner he's in the store.

00:10:13.227 --> 00:10:14.157
"He didn't do this.

00:10:14.157 --> 00:10:15.730
"What are you talking about?"

00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:19.330
So he was like, he was
adamant that he wasn't trying

00:10:19.330 --> 00:10:21.547
to do anything but arrest
me that particular day.

00:10:21.547 --> 00:10:25.000
And I really, even as I think back now

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:28.100
and I sit and I remember
how that whole scenario

00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:32.330
played out, and the
fact that I didn't know,

00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:34.740
I had no idea what was going on.

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:37.040
And then him not wanting to take me

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:38.990
into the store for the owner

00:10:38.990 --> 00:10:42.300
of the store to verify
that I was not the person,

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:44.160
this only inflamed things more.

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:47.870
So a fellow that was standing
there named Harrison Conyers,

00:10:47.870 --> 00:10:51.000
I never forget Harrison and
I grew up on Kent Street,

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:53.850
that's where I lived at on Kent Street

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:55.160
in the Westford project.

00:10:55.160 --> 00:10:57.267
He said, "Leslie, I'm gonna tell your mom

00:10:57.267 --> 00:11:00.160
"I'm go tell Ms. Clara,
the police arrested you."

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:01.513
My mother's name was Clara.

00:11:02.590 --> 00:11:04.187
So he said, "I'm gonna go tell Miss Clara

00:11:04.187 --> 00:11:05.940
"that they arrested you and so forth."

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:07.117
And I'm like, "Okay."

00:11:08.494 --> 00:11:09.610
But all the while I'm scared to death

00:11:09.610 --> 00:11:12.420
because I don't know what is going on.

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:14.148
This is not something that,

00:11:14.148 --> 00:11:16.131
this is not a everyday thing for me.

00:11:16.131 --> 00:11:18.680
I'd never been stopped by the police,

00:11:18.680 --> 00:11:22.530
I had never been accosted in any kind,

00:11:22.530 --> 00:11:25.950
I never had any kind of
interaction with the police.

00:11:25.950 --> 00:11:29.910
And to be honest with you, I was a child,

00:11:29.910 --> 00:11:34.910
I was 17 years of age, but
I was a child in mentality.

00:11:35.670 --> 00:11:39.283
I had no understanding
of what was transpiring.

00:11:41.370 --> 00:11:46.370
I had no knowledge of what
was going to procedures

00:11:46.850 --> 00:11:48.840
that was happening.

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:50.570
The only thing I know is
that I'm standing here,

00:11:50.570 --> 00:11:52.660
this man with has gun against my head,

00:11:52.660 --> 00:11:54.660
and if I move he can kill me.

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:56.153
That's all I could think of.

00:11:57.700 --> 00:12:01.849
When the police wagon came and
he put me in a police wagon,

00:12:01.849 --> 00:12:03.320
they carted me to the station.

00:12:03.320 --> 00:12:07.070
I remember when I got there
and they took me out the wagon,

00:12:07.070 --> 00:12:09.730
and officers said, "What's your name?"

00:12:09.730 --> 00:12:11.160
And I said, "Leslie Vass."

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:13.130
And he said, "How old are you?"

00:12:13.130 --> 00:12:14.500
I said, "17."

00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:16.500
He says, "17!"

00:12:16.500 --> 00:12:19.590
He said, "Sarge, we got a juvenile here."

00:12:19.590 --> 00:12:22.273
He has to be sequestered
from everybody else.

00:12:24.064 --> 00:12:26.680
Meaning they couldn't put
me with other individuals.

00:12:26.680 --> 00:12:29.170
So he put me in a, they
walked me back and put me

00:12:29.170 --> 00:12:33.110
in this area where it was just the cells,

00:12:33.110 --> 00:12:35.560
and I was, he put me in the first cell

00:12:35.560 --> 00:12:38.583
and he put a tag on there
saying that I was a juvenile.

00:12:39.900 --> 00:12:41.290
And the guard, I remember, the officers,

00:12:41.290 --> 00:12:42.917
when they called Sarge, he said,

00:12:42.917 --> 00:12:46.420
"Wow, he a big kid for a 17-year old, boy"

00:12:46.420 --> 00:12:49.083
I stood six, four at that time,

00:12:51.139 --> 00:12:55.300
but still I just, you know,

00:12:56.524 --> 00:12:57.357
as I try to

00:12:59.930 --> 00:13:01.980
go back over everything that happened,

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:06.530
I just feel like, you
know, it's truly traumatic

00:13:06.530 --> 00:13:10.000
because I didn't understand
what was going on.

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:15.000
I had no idea that I
was being charged as a,

00:13:15.110 --> 00:13:19.120
I was a juvenile, but I was
being charged as an adult.

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:23.830
Then later on, they came and got me,

00:13:23.830 --> 00:13:26.920
and told me that I had to
see a court commissioner,

00:13:26.920 --> 00:13:29.593
which they call the initial appearance.

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:33.010
And when I went before this man

00:13:33.010 --> 00:13:34.960
they cuffed me in the courtroom,

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:36.043
I saw my mother.

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:40.240
And when I went into the
courtroom, I could see her.

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:44.630
I saw her friend, Mr.
Johnny sitting with her

00:13:46.267 --> 00:13:47.720
and my neighbor, Miss Mick

00:13:47.720 --> 00:13:51.103
which was a friend like
an aunt to me right.

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:55.518
They was telling me just to hold on

00:13:55.518 --> 00:13:57.550
that it'll be alright and stuff.

00:13:57.550 --> 00:14:01.997
And then the judge said,
"Well, we will release you

00:14:01.997 --> 00:14:04.907
"into the custody of your mother
because you're a juvenile,

00:14:04.907 --> 00:14:07.940
"you don't have a prior arrest record."

00:14:07.940 --> 00:14:11.487
He said, I remember him
saying, "But I'm telling you

00:14:11.487 --> 00:14:15.837
"if you go over to the pharmacy

00:14:15.837 --> 00:14:20.297
"or you talk to anybody
that work at this pharmacy,

00:14:20.297 --> 00:14:23.417
"and it comes back to the
courts, that's a violation

00:14:23.417 --> 00:14:26.647
"and you will be rearrested
and held without bail."

00:14:28.430 --> 00:14:30.540
All these things is foreign to me

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:32.853
'cause I don't understand, what is a bail?

00:14:33.819 --> 00:14:36.543
I don't have no idea about
none of these things.

00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:40.823
They released me.

00:14:42.160 --> 00:14:43.820
And as we was walking to the car,

00:14:43.820 --> 00:14:45.217
I was trying to tell my mom, I said,

00:14:45.217 --> 00:14:47.197
"Look," I said, "I don't know

00:14:47.197 --> 00:14:49.460
"what these people talking about."

00:14:49.460 --> 00:14:50.640
And she said, "Don't worry about it.

00:14:50.640 --> 00:14:52.557
"I'ma get a lawyer for you.

00:14:52.557 --> 00:14:55.247
"You do what the lawyer tell you to do.

00:14:55.247 --> 00:14:57.127
"That's all I want you to do.

00:14:57.127 --> 00:14:59.907
"Don't go over there near
that drugstore, Leslie.

00:14:59.907 --> 00:15:02.177
"And don't talk to Mr.
Clarence, and don't talk

00:15:02.177 --> 00:15:04.237
"to none of these other
people that work there

00:15:04.237 --> 00:15:08.380
"because they gonna lock
you back up if you do."

00:15:08.380 --> 00:15:13.170
That's all I could, that's
all I remember, lock up.

00:15:13.170 --> 00:15:17.723
If I talk to anybody, if I go
over there to that drug store.

00:15:20.030 --> 00:15:22.120
Those were the things
that stuck in my mind.

00:15:22.120 --> 00:15:25.950
First of all, I was at a cell by myself,

00:15:25.950 --> 00:15:29.660
that I could put my, spread my arms out

00:15:29.660 --> 00:15:32.980
and put my hands on each side
of the balls, where I was at

00:15:32.980 --> 00:15:36.677
with the little toilet and
the thing and a sink, right.

00:15:38.752 --> 00:15:39.980
And I ain't trying to be there,

00:15:39.980 --> 00:15:41.403
that's not where I wanna be.

00:15:44.050 --> 00:15:45.980
She paid this attorney.

00:15:45.980 --> 00:15:48.010
She was referred to this attorney

00:15:48.010 --> 00:15:51.093
by the name of Robert Paul Conrad.

00:15:52.760 --> 00:15:55.810
And when we first met him,

00:15:55.810 --> 00:15:58.727
she made it specifically clear to me.

00:15:58.727 --> 00:16:01.797
"You do what this lawyer tell you to do.

00:16:01.797 --> 00:16:04.107
"I don't care what.

00:16:04.107 --> 00:16:06.337
"I'm paying him, he's the lawyer.

00:16:06.337 --> 00:16:08.017
"He knows about the law.

00:16:08.017 --> 00:16:09.853
"You don't know anything about it.

00:16:12.100 --> 00:16:13.537
"And you do what he say to do."

00:16:14.824 --> 00:16:17.413
I'm a kid, I do what
my mom tells me to do.

00:16:18.550 --> 00:16:21.360
The first time that I met him without her,

00:16:21.360 --> 00:16:22.847
the first thing he said to me was,

00:16:22.847 --> 00:16:26.100
"Did your mother send me
a check or an envelope?"

00:16:26.100 --> 00:16:28.920
So I said, "Yeah, she gave me
an envelope to give to you."

00:16:28.920 --> 00:16:30.710
I give him the envelope.

00:16:30.710 --> 00:16:32.567
And he started saying to me,

00:16:32.567 --> 00:16:35.857
"Well we're gonna have a hearing

00:16:35.857 --> 00:16:38.007
"and when we go have the hearing

00:16:38.007 --> 00:16:41.230
"I want you to listen to
everything that I tell you.'

00:16:41.230 --> 00:16:42.740
And I'm saying, "Okay."

00:16:42.740 --> 00:16:44.577
I said, "But sir," I said

00:16:44.577 --> 00:16:47.037
"They said that I've robbed a drug store.

00:16:47.037 --> 00:16:49.547
"I know the guy that owned the drug store.

00:16:49.547 --> 00:16:52.087
"I know Mr. Clarence, I
know Mr. Willie Adams,

00:16:52.087 --> 00:16:53.117
"Old Man Willie.

00:16:53.117 --> 00:16:56.015
"I know Old Man Willie, I know Clarence."

00:16:56.015 --> 00:16:57.677
I said, "I know some of the other workers

00:16:57.677 --> 00:16:58.680
"that work in the store."

00:16:58.680 --> 00:17:00.077
I said, "Man, these people can tell you

00:17:00.077 --> 00:17:01.963
"I didn't rob that place."

00:17:02.960 --> 00:17:04.549
And he just looking at me, he said,

00:17:04.549 --> 00:17:05.720
"Don't worry about that."

00:17:05.720 --> 00:17:07.400
He said, "We will take care of this.

00:17:07.400 --> 00:17:09.900
"It's not gonna be a problem, okay."

00:17:09.900 --> 00:17:11.050
So I said, "All right."

00:17:14.313 --> 00:17:16.406
I went about doing what I was doing,

00:17:16.406 --> 00:17:17.806
I was still going to school.

00:17:20.027 --> 00:17:23.530
I remember one time

00:17:23.530 --> 00:17:26.500
I got a paper that said I
supposedly went to court,

00:17:26.500 --> 00:17:29.420
but when my mom called
to talk to the lawyer

00:17:29.420 --> 00:17:30.253
he said, "Don't worry about it

00:17:30.253 --> 00:17:32.327
"the case is gonna be dismissed."

00:17:34.407 --> 00:17:35.767
"It's gonna be postponed.

00:17:35.767 --> 00:17:37.737
"And when I ask you to go to court

00:17:37.737 --> 00:17:39.597
"it's gonna be going for trial."

00:17:43.120 --> 00:17:47.450
I don't know anything other
than what's been said to me,

00:17:47.450 --> 00:17:50.417
that I'm charged with
robbing a store, right.

00:17:51.300 --> 00:17:55.440
I was arrested February 15th, 1975

00:17:55.440 --> 00:17:58.443
on Annapolis Road, after
walking out of the drugstore.

00:18:00.354 --> 00:18:05.354
July 2nd, 1975 I appeared
at the then Supreme Bench

00:18:05.770 --> 00:18:09.920
for Baltimore City at
111 North Calvert Street

00:18:09.920 --> 00:18:11.803
in Baltimore City for court.

00:18:13.050 --> 00:18:15.960
When I got there, the
doors to the courtroom

00:18:15.960 --> 00:18:18.400
were actually locked, and people

00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:20.180
were just standing around outside

00:18:20.180 --> 00:18:23.210
waiting for the courtroom
doors to be opened.

00:18:23.210 --> 00:18:27.560
So as I was standing
there, I see Mr. Clarence

00:18:27.560 --> 00:18:31.980
I see Old Man Willie Adams,
and I see this other dude,

00:18:31.980 --> 00:18:34.400
and they're all standing
around this white guy

00:18:34.400 --> 00:18:37.453
with a yellow manila folder.

00:18:39.738 --> 00:18:42.680
And they talk and the Mr. Clarence look up

00:18:42.680 --> 00:18:46.297
and he see me standing over
there and he said to me

00:18:46.297 --> 00:18:50.667
"Leslie what are you doing out here?"

00:18:52.040 --> 00:18:54.647
My immediate thought was,

00:18:54.647 --> 00:18:55.697
"These people will lock you up

00:18:55.697 --> 00:18:58.780
"if you talk to this man, you understand?"

00:18:58.780 --> 00:19:01.110
That's my first and foremost thought,

00:19:01.110 --> 00:19:03.000
so I don't want to say nothing to him.

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:05.800
So he's like, "What's wrong with you?"

00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:07.153
I bust out crying,

00:19:08.440 --> 00:19:10.727
and I say to him, I said,
"They gonna lock me up

00:19:10.727 --> 00:19:12.180
"if I talk to you."

00:19:12.180 --> 00:19:14.980
He said, "What are you
talking about Leslie?"

00:19:14.980 --> 00:19:17.817
I said, "They charged me
with robbing a drug store."

00:19:20.930 --> 00:19:23.327
He said to me, he said, "Wait a minute.

00:19:23.327 --> 00:19:25.750
"What, when did that happen?"

00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:30.157
I said, "February and when
I came in to drugstore

00:19:30.157 --> 00:19:34.007
"to buy my mama paper,
as I leave out of there,

00:19:34.007 --> 00:19:36.860
"they arrest me and said,
I robbed a drug store."

00:19:36.860 --> 00:19:40.707
He said, "They lock you up for that?"

00:19:41.792 --> 00:19:42.660
I see "Yeah."

00:19:42.660 --> 00:19:46.487
So Mr. Willie is standing
there and he says,

00:19:46.487 --> 00:19:48.619
"Man what the hell is going,

00:19:48.619 --> 00:19:50.990
excuse me, he said, "What
the hell is going on here?"

00:19:50.990 --> 00:19:52.220
And they looking at each other,

00:19:52.220 --> 00:19:55.740
like it's something, they
know that I'm not familiar.

00:19:55.740 --> 00:19:57.340
I don't know anything about.

00:19:57.340 --> 00:19:59.777
So he said, "Leslie,
come on and go with me,

00:19:59.777 --> 00:20:01.438
"walk over here."

00:20:01.438 --> 00:20:03.980
So he walks me over to the white guy

00:20:03.980 --> 00:20:07.630
with the yellow folder and
he says to him, he said

00:20:08.580 --> 00:20:13.203
"Y'all lock this kid up,
this is who y'all locked up?"

00:20:13.203 --> 00:20:15.617
He said, "We know this
child, this kid come from

00:20:15.617 --> 00:20:17.227
"out of Westport community.

00:20:17.227 --> 00:20:19.757
"This kid wasn't involved
in this robbery."

00:20:22.450 --> 00:20:23.620
He said, "Something is wrong."

00:20:23.620 --> 00:20:25.860
He said, "This is not the kid."

00:20:25.860 --> 00:20:30.860
He said, "The people that robbed
our co-worker were older."

00:20:32.010 --> 00:20:34.477
He said, "We know this kid, this kid

00:20:34.477 --> 00:20:35.727
"has been coming to the store

00:20:35.727 --> 00:20:38.157
"since he was a small little boy."

00:20:39.180 --> 00:20:44.180
He said, "You got the wrong
person, not this boy."

00:20:45.520 --> 00:20:48.167
So the guy, I remember the
guy saying to him, he said,

00:20:48.167 --> 00:20:51.127
"Well, guess what we
don't need your testimony.

00:20:51.127 --> 00:20:53.707
"We're not gonna use
y'all, you dismissed."

00:20:55.680 --> 00:20:57.657
And Mr. Willie said, "You know what,

00:20:57.657 --> 00:21:00.070
"these people gonna do
some real fucked up shit."

00:21:00.070 --> 00:21:01.070
That's what he said.

00:21:02.150 --> 00:21:04.910
And I mean that's exactly
how he said it right there

00:21:04.910 --> 00:21:07.860
in the hallway, outside of the courtroom.

00:21:07.860 --> 00:21:09.430
Then they opened the doors

00:21:09.430 --> 00:21:11.830
to the courtroom and
everybody started going in.

00:21:13.671 --> 00:21:16.411
So as I'm going in, Mr. Willie said,

00:21:16.411 --> 00:21:18.780
"Where's your lawyer,
Leslie, who is your lawyer?"

00:21:18.780 --> 00:21:21.080
So I said, "I don't see him right now."

00:21:21.080 --> 00:21:22.540
I said, "Oh, there he go."

00:21:22.540 --> 00:21:24.070
He had just came up the steps.

00:21:24.070 --> 00:21:27.130
I said, "He already talked to
that man you always talk to."

00:21:27.130 --> 00:21:29.320
And they look at each other
and then they looked back at me

00:21:29.320 --> 00:21:33.280
and they said, "Leslie, you
know, where's your mom."

00:21:33.280 --> 00:21:36.483
'Cause they knew that my
mother was a single mother.

00:21:39.059 --> 00:21:42.000
These people at this time
were delivery drivers

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:45.250
who came into the community to deliver

00:21:45.250 --> 00:21:47.690
food, medication, alcohol

00:21:47.690 --> 00:21:50.070
so there was a bar in
the back of this place,

00:21:50.070 --> 00:21:53.160
and so they were friendly
with all the people

00:21:53.160 --> 00:21:55.463
in the community, you know.

00:21:58.434 --> 00:21:59.334
I'm standing there

00:22:00.452 --> 00:22:04.910
and apparently that
man had told Mr. Conrad

00:22:04.910 --> 00:22:08.920
that I was talking to the two witnesses

00:22:08.920 --> 00:22:11.940
and they had said
something to him about it.

00:22:11.940 --> 00:22:15.290
And he came, when he
approached me, he approached me

00:22:15.290 --> 00:22:20.080
like, "Didn't I tell you not
to talk to nobody about that?'

00:22:20.080 --> 00:22:21.350
I don't care who they are.

00:22:21.350 --> 00:22:26.100
I said, "Well, they told
him that I didn't do this."

00:22:26.100 --> 00:22:27.357
He said, "I don't care what they said.

00:22:27.357 --> 00:22:29.547
"I told you, your mother told you to do

00:22:29.547 --> 00:22:30.727
"what I tell you to do.

00:22:30.727 --> 00:22:32.147
"That's what you're supposed to do.

00:22:32.147 --> 00:22:33.447
"Don't talk to nobody.

00:22:33.447 --> 00:22:35.600
"Go there, have a seat."

00:22:35.600 --> 00:22:37.760
And I'm saying, I'm trying
to figure out why he upset

00:22:37.760 --> 00:22:41.600
with me and mad with me because
I talked to these people?

00:22:41.600 --> 00:22:44.680
And these people apparently know something

00:22:44.680 --> 00:22:46.263
about what was going on.

00:22:49.575 --> 00:22:51.222
Now, when they, I'm
sitting in the I'm sitting

00:22:51.222 --> 00:22:52.513
in the courtroom,

00:22:53.690 --> 00:22:57.420
I remember I heard a movement behind me

00:22:57.420 --> 00:23:00.390
but my mind is so messed
up about being here

00:23:00.390 --> 00:23:02.373
I don't understand what's going on.

00:23:03.995 --> 00:23:06.217
And then I'm thinking, you know,

00:23:06.217 --> 00:23:11.217
"Why is this lawyer, my
lawyer mad at me for talking

00:23:11.247 --> 00:23:13.927
"with these people who
work in the drugstore?"

00:23:15.729 --> 00:23:18.977
I said, when he come over, I said,

00:23:18.977 --> 00:23:21.797
"Mr. Conrad" I said,
"they saying something

00:23:21.797 --> 00:23:25.077
"about somebody that was a delivery driver

00:23:25.077 --> 00:23:26.930
"that was robbed now."

00:23:26.930 --> 00:23:29.960
I said, "But they charged me
with robbing the drug store.

00:23:29.960 --> 00:23:32.580
They didn't send nothing
about no delivery driver.

00:23:32.580 --> 00:23:35.307
So he said, "You don't know,

00:23:35.307 --> 00:23:37.930
"you never see a person
that pick you out."

00:23:37.930 --> 00:23:39.068
I said, "Nobody picked me out.

00:23:39.068 --> 00:23:41.430
"Pick me out for what?"

00:23:41.430 --> 00:23:45.107
He said, "Leslie, the day
that you was arrested.

00:23:45.107 --> 00:23:49.720
"Did anybody say that you robbed them?"

00:23:49.720 --> 00:23:51.560
And I said, "No, not that I remember."

00:23:51.560 --> 00:23:52.737
I said, "The only thing I remember

00:23:52.737 --> 00:23:55.540
"was that the officer
had the gun to my head,"

00:23:55.540 --> 00:23:58.713
and I said, "Oh, I
remember hearing a voice,

00:23:59.687 --> 00:24:02.907
"the officer saying to
somebody, 'Is this the man?'

00:24:02.907 --> 00:24:05.675
"And he said, 'Yeah, it looks like him,'

00:24:05.675 --> 00:24:07.227
"and then that was it.

00:24:07.227 --> 00:24:10.040
"But there's nothing else said, right."

00:24:10.040 --> 00:24:12.100
So I said, "I don't know."

00:24:12.100 --> 00:24:13.410
I said, "What did that suppose mean?"

00:24:13.410 --> 00:24:18.410
He said, "Well, so you don't
know that the robbery happened

00:24:18.797 --> 00:24:23.797
"in November of 1974 in Cherry Hill."

00:24:25.280 --> 00:24:28.807
So I said, "Cherry Hill,
what are you talking about?

00:24:28.807 --> 00:24:30.527
"I was arrested on Annapolis Road

00:24:30.527 --> 00:24:33.207
"right in front of the Westport Pharmacy."

00:24:33.207 --> 00:24:34.500
"Maybe they got me."

00:24:34.500 --> 00:24:38.017
He said, "No Leslie the
original crime happened,

00:24:38.017 --> 00:24:42.733
"November 2nd, 1974, a
part-time delivery driver

00:24:42.733 --> 00:24:45.740
"for the Westport Pharmacy
was making a delivery."

00:24:45.740 --> 00:24:47.750
So I said, "I don't know
anything about none of this."

00:24:47.750 --> 00:24:49.870
I said, "This is the first
time I'm hearing it."

00:24:49.870 --> 00:24:53.040
I said, "You telling me
something I have no idea about."

00:24:53.040 --> 00:24:54.727
So I said, "If it was on
a weekend or something

00:24:54.727 --> 00:24:56.947
"usually I'm on at my grandfather's house

00:24:56.947 --> 00:25:00.807
"because on Fridays, we
had basketball practice,

00:25:00.807 --> 00:25:03.007
"I leave there and go to
work at Muhly's Bakery,

00:25:03.007 --> 00:25:04.927
"my grandfather lived
right around the corner

00:25:04.927 --> 00:25:07.921
"on Hanover Street, and
I used to go there.'

00:25:07.921 --> 00:25:10.037
"And after work at the bakery

00:25:10.037 --> 00:25:12.057
"because it was late in the evening

00:25:12.057 --> 00:25:15.697
"and didn't no public transportation run

00:25:15.697 --> 00:25:18.977
"after a certain period of
time where we lived at."

00:25:19.960 --> 00:25:23.510
So he said, "Well, don't worry about it."

00:25:23.510 --> 00:25:26.370
So I said, "Well who was this
part-time delivery driver?"

00:25:26.370 --> 00:25:29.127
I said, "Because Mr. Clarence I'd know,

00:25:29.127 --> 00:25:32.037
"Willie Adams I'd know, you understand.

00:25:32.037 --> 00:25:34.597
"I don't know of no other
delivery drivers that work

00:25:34.597 --> 00:25:35.897
"for the Westport Pharmacy.

00:25:35.897 --> 00:25:37.350
"I've never known of any."

00:25:37.350 --> 00:25:40.117
So he said, "Well, this man only worked

00:25:40.117 --> 00:25:42.647
"one day a week on a Saturday,

00:25:42.647 --> 00:25:45.447
"and he originally worked for
the gas and electric company,

00:25:45.447 --> 00:25:48.910
"but he just fills and he
do part-time work there.'

00:25:48.910 --> 00:25:50.267
So I said, "Who is he?

00:25:50.267 --> 00:25:51.817
"Where's he at?

00:25:51.817 --> 00:25:53.980
"And he identified me?"

00:25:53.980 --> 00:25:57.247
And he said, "Don't look now, Leslie,

00:25:57.247 --> 00:26:01.527
"but the man sitting behind
you with the red plaid,

00:26:01.527 --> 00:26:04.847
"look like a lumberjack
shirt on, that's the man."

00:26:05.933 --> 00:26:09.707
And when he said it, I immediately
turned around and look.

00:26:09.707 --> 00:26:12.930
But when I looked back, I
noticed that the white fellow

00:26:12.930 --> 00:26:16.700
had a folder, a yellow manila folder open,

00:26:16.700 --> 00:26:20.470
and in that folder was my
picture when I was arrested

00:26:20.470 --> 00:26:23.120
with my bush and all over my head

00:26:23.120 --> 00:26:26.530
because I didn't have my
hair braided or plaited,

00:26:26.530 --> 00:26:29.220
and it was all over my
head in the picture.

00:26:29.220 --> 00:26:30.520
And I was like, "Well, why?"

00:26:30.520 --> 00:26:32.837
I said, "He's showing him picture of me."

00:26:34.380 --> 00:26:36.510
So he said, "Leslie," he
said, "just wait here."

00:26:36.510 --> 00:26:39.400
He turned around and said
something to the fellow

00:26:39.400 --> 00:26:41.600
without knowing it was
the state prosecutor.

00:26:42.503 --> 00:26:44.530
And they up and stepped to the side

00:26:44.530 --> 00:26:45.830
and was talking and stuff.

00:26:46.840 --> 00:26:51.560
Then the state prosecutor
walked up to the,

00:26:51.560 --> 00:26:54.490
I think the clerk's desk and
said something to the clerk,

00:26:54.490 --> 00:26:56.820
and then they said, they called the case

00:26:56.820 --> 00:26:59.273
of State of Maryland versus Leslie Vass.

00:27:01.090 --> 00:27:05.120
So Mr. Clarence and Willie Adams

00:27:06.020 --> 00:27:09.160
asked to speak to my attorney, Mr. Conrad,

00:27:09.160 --> 00:27:11.720
he told them that he couldn't talk to them

00:27:11.720 --> 00:27:14.110
because they were listed
as state witnesses

00:27:15.390 --> 00:27:16.650
and it would be illegal to talk to them.

00:27:16.650 --> 00:27:18.727
I don't know the procedure.

00:27:18.727 --> 00:27:21.590
I only thing I could go by what I remember

00:27:21.590 --> 00:27:24.170
of this case, I didn't find out

00:27:24.170 --> 00:27:26.350
or know anything about the procedures

00:27:26.350 --> 00:27:30.150
of criminal law until after
I was convicted and in prison

00:27:30.150 --> 00:27:32.663
for 20-year sentence at the age of 17.

00:27:35.970 --> 00:27:38.263
This man, the victim,

00:27:39.764 --> 00:27:42.977
they told, when they
called my case, they said,

00:27:42.977 --> 00:27:46.997
"Leslie Vass, the State of
Maryland versus Leslie Vass.

00:27:46.997 --> 00:27:51.300
"Mr. Vass come up to the defense table."

00:27:51.300 --> 00:27:54.060
And then the lawyer
started walking up there.

00:27:54.060 --> 00:27:55.527
Then the prosecutor said,

00:27:55.527 --> 00:27:57.317
"Your honor and we're asking

00:27:57.317 --> 00:28:00.857
"that the witness be sequestered,

00:28:00.857 --> 00:28:04.817
"the victim be sequestered
from the courtroom."

00:28:06.810 --> 00:28:11.050
And he gets up from behind me and walks,

00:28:11.050 --> 00:28:12.860
they walked him to the door

00:28:12.860 --> 00:28:15.323
and tell him to go
outside and wait outside.

00:28:16.290 --> 00:28:21.290
Then they say, "Go sit anywhere
in the courtroom Leslie

00:28:21.527 --> 00:28:24.967
"because this is gonna be
identification process."

00:28:26.470 --> 00:28:27.730
Now wasn't that dumb?

00:28:27.730 --> 00:28:31.010
He was just sitting
behind me you understand?

00:28:31.010 --> 00:28:33.400
So why do you need identification process,

00:28:33.400 --> 00:28:35.360
when they showed him a
picture and he's sitting

00:28:35.360 --> 00:28:36.193
right behind me?

00:28:36.193 --> 00:28:38.007
Now you're gonna tell me
to go sit in the courtroom

00:28:38.007 --> 00:28:41.517
and then he comes in
and then they asked him,

00:28:41.517 --> 00:28:44.370
"Well, did you see the man, the third man?

00:28:44.370 --> 00:28:45.940
He ain't say, "Do you see the man?"

00:28:45.940 --> 00:28:49.497
They said, "Do you see the
third man that was involved

00:28:49.497 --> 00:28:53.765
"in the robbery of you
on November 2nd, 1974?"

00:28:53.765 --> 00:28:57.520
And I'm all the while when
they're saying this number,

00:28:57.520 --> 00:28:59.600
November 2nd, 1974,

00:28:59.600 --> 00:29:02.787
I'm saying, "I was arrested
February 15th, 1975.

00:29:02.787 --> 00:29:05.557
"I don't know nothing about no November."

00:29:09.370 --> 00:29:13.050
I mean it's like, ever
since I got convicted

00:29:13.050 --> 00:29:13.883
of this crime,

00:29:15.670 --> 00:29:19.020
I've always had real serious problems

00:29:19.020 --> 00:29:22.400
about dealing with how it was done

00:29:23.700 --> 00:29:27.310
because I felt like
these people, they were

00:29:27.310 --> 00:29:30.790
after being in prison
for as long as I have,

00:29:30.790 --> 00:29:33.870
as I was and then finding out the evidence

00:29:33.870 --> 00:29:36.653
this things that actually
happened in my case,

00:29:37.510 --> 00:29:39.670
all of it boils down to the fact

00:29:39.670 --> 00:29:43.230
that the detective that was
in charge of investigating

00:29:43.230 --> 00:29:47.860
robbery was aware that the
fellow I was mistaken for

00:29:47.860 --> 00:29:50.743
was a informant for them,

00:29:51.690 --> 00:29:56.690
and they actually allowed me
to go to prison to protect him.

00:29:58.350 --> 00:30:00.473
Yeah, yeah, really.

00:30:03.460 --> 00:30:08.460
Judge, when it came time
for us to select a trial,

00:30:12.320 --> 00:30:15.177
the lawyer, Mr. Conrad said to me,

00:30:15.177 --> 00:30:17.997
"Leslie you suppose, when they asked you

00:30:17.997 --> 00:30:19.307
"what kind of trial you have,

00:30:19.307 --> 00:30:22.280
"I want you to say court trial."

00:30:22.280 --> 00:30:24.607
So I said, "Well, my mother
says I supposed to have a trial

00:30:24.607 --> 00:30:27.070
"by jury, what's that?"

00:30:27.070 --> 00:30:29.517
He said, "And she also told you to do

00:30:29.517 --> 00:30:31.620
"what I tell you to do."

00:30:31.620 --> 00:30:34.410
He told me to ask for a trial by court.

00:30:34.410 --> 00:30:35.877
And I said, "Well my mother said

00:30:35.877 --> 00:30:37.300
"it was it was supposed trial by jury."

00:30:37.300 --> 00:30:40.180
He said, "No, I'm telling you
to ask for trial by court.

00:30:40.180 --> 00:30:45.180
So when they asked me, I
said, "Okay, trial by jury,"

00:30:45.310 --> 00:30:46.483
'cause he said that.

00:30:47.340 --> 00:30:50.167
And he said, "Is that what
you want a trial by jury,

00:30:50.167 --> 00:30:51.880
"No, trial by court?"

00:30:51.880 --> 00:30:53.970
So I said, "Yeah trial by court,"

00:30:53.970 --> 00:30:55.323
'cause I was mixed up.

00:30:56.668 --> 00:30:57.970
And then he said, "Okay."

00:30:59.446 --> 00:31:02.267
They supposedly presented their case,

00:31:02.267 --> 00:31:04.040
and this man got on the stand

00:31:04.040 --> 00:31:06.140
and started telling about how three men,

00:31:06.140 --> 00:31:07.510
he went to make a delivery,

00:31:07.510 --> 00:31:12.410
three men approached
him and robbed him $120.

00:31:12.410 --> 00:31:15.313
Three men robbed him for $120.

00:31:17.890 --> 00:31:21.940
He started explaining how he
left when they robbed him.

00:31:21.940 --> 00:31:23.710
He got in the car and left and went

00:31:23.710 --> 00:31:26.873
to the gas station and called
the police to report it

00:31:26.873 --> 00:31:27.770
because there were no cell phones

00:31:27.770 --> 00:31:29.520
and other stuff at the time.

00:31:29.520 --> 00:31:30.890
And they met him out there,

00:31:30.890 --> 00:31:31.767
the police met him out there

00:31:31.767 --> 00:31:34.113
and he explained to him
what happened and all.

00:31:36.315 --> 00:31:39.380
Later on that evening,

00:31:39.380 --> 00:31:42.420
his coworkers, Mr. Willie Adams

00:31:42.420 --> 00:31:46.050
and Clarence McCleary went
with him back out there.

00:31:46.050 --> 00:31:48.680
and they observed three men

00:31:48.680 --> 00:31:52.870
with whom he identified as the
men who actually robbed him

00:31:52.870 --> 00:31:57.000
get into a yellow Volkswagen
with black stripes on it.

00:31:57.000 --> 00:32:00.230
Mr. Clarence and them
went to the gas station

00:32:00.230 --> 00:32:03.370
to use the phone to call
the police and gave them

00:32:05.030 --> 00:32:09.230
the car tag number and the
description of the vehicle.

00:32:09.230 --> 00:32:11.810
An all-points bulletin was put out,

00:32:11.810 --> 00:32:15.800
the vehicle was stopped and
two men were in the vehicle

00:32:15.800 --> 00:32:19.840
admitted committing a robbery,
third man, wasn't in there,

00:32:19.840 --> 00:32:22.053
wasn't a third person in there, right.

00:32:23.080 --> 00:32:26.920
Based on that information and
his in-court identification

00:32:26.920 --> 00:32:28.840
I was found guilty

00:32:28.840 --> 00:32:33.840
and I was detained with a $20,000 bail.

00:32:37.690 --> 00:32:39.480
That was July 2nd, 1975.

00:32:39.480 --> 00:32:42.450
July 18th they brought me back to court

00:32:42.450 --> 00:32:46.280
and they sentenced me to
15 years, plus five years

00:32:46.280 --> 00:32:49.890
consecutive with the 15
makes it a total of 20 years

00:32:49.890 --> 00:32:51.730
for armed robbery.

00:32:51.730 --> 00:32:55.120
And I entered into the
Maryland State prison system

00:32:55.120 --> 00:32:56.663
the Division of Corrections.

00:32:57.720 --> 00:32:59.670
When I went into the
Division of Corrections

00:32:59.670 --> 00:33:01.930
I ended up in Maryland Penitentiary

00:33:01.930 --> 00:33:04.200
which was a maximum security facility

00:33:04.200 --> 00:33:05.250
in the State of Maryland.

00:33:05.250 --> 00:33:07.000
- [Mark] So you are no
longer a juvenile now?

00:33:07.000 --> 00:33:09.240
- No, I was an adult.

00:33:09.240 --> 00:33:10.830
They made me an adult automatically.

00:33:10.830 --> 00:33:13.720
Never had a juvenile
waiver hearing anything,

00:33:13.720 --> 00:33:15.580
they immediately just processed me

00:33:15.580 --> 00:33:17.810
and that's something that I went through

00:33:17.810 --> 00:33:19.830
when I was confined, because I started,

00:33:19.830 --> 00:33:22.230
I met a guy that was on the tier above me,

00:33:22.230 --> 00:33:23.970
which was death row.

00:33:23.970 --> 00:33:27.900
And he introduced me to
how to learn the procedures

00:33:27.900 --> 00:33:29.740
of doing legal work.

00:33:29.740 --> 00:33:33.103
And I ended up, started
studying paralegal,

00:33:33.103 --> 00:33:35.613
study paralegal, to sort of,

00:33:37.090 --> 00:33:39.170
doing my own work on my own case.

00:33:39.170 --> 00:33:41.830
I started obtaining the
documents related to my case,

00:33:41.830 --> 00:33:44.770
I received the original
police officer's report

00:33:44.770 --> 00:33:45.960
and all that stuff.

00:33:45.960 --> 00:33:49.310
In the course of me being confined

00:33:49.310 --> 00:33:52.820
I actually became known as an individual

00:33:52.820 --> 00:33:55.290
who was learned in law right.

00:33:55.290 --> 00:33:57.950
I worked with a lot of individuals,

00:33:57.950 --> 00:33:59.900
worked on other individuals cases.

00:33:59.900 --> 00:34:02.430
I ended up getting cases overturned,

00:34:02.430 --> 00:34:05.583
sentencing reductions and
all that kind of stuff.

00:34:09.560 --> 00:34:13.907
And 1980, I actually
learned the name of a guy

00:34:15.382 --> 00:34:19.320
that was a real close
friend of those two men

00:34:19.320 --> 00:34:22.640
that's supposed to have
been my co-defendants

00:34:22.640 --> 00:34:24.400
that I had never met.

00:34:24.400 --> 00:34:26.970
And I asked this lawyer whether or not

00:34:26.970 --> 00:34:30.550
he would get a photograph of that man

00:34:30.550 --> 00:34:33.043
and show it to the victim to see,

00:34:33.043 --> 00:34:35.783
whether or not this was the man
I was actually mistaken for?

00:34:37.070 --> 00:34:40.320
This man was the person that I ended up

00:34:40.320 --> 00:34:42.723
getting a picture of in 1982.

00:34:43.670 --> 00:34:45.810
And at the time I had a petition

00:34:45.810 --> 00:34:48.740
in federal court 'cause
I got tired of dealing

00:34:48.740 --> 00:34:50.350
with the state court 'cause I realized

00:34:50.350 --> 00:34:52.520
they wasn't gonna give me anything.

00:34:52.520 --> 00:34:54.490
They weren't gonna adhere to the laws

00:34:54.490 --> 00:34:56.180
like they supposed to.

00:34:56.180 --> 00:35:00.670
Nowhere in the world I was
supposed to be tried as an adult,

00:35:00.670 --> 00:35:03.939
and I was a juvenile
without a waiver hearing.

00:35:03.939 --> 00:35:07.860
That's not permissible by law period,

00:35:07.860 --> 00:35:10.920
but the State of Maryland
kept denying my petition.

00:35:10.920 --> 00:35:15.750
So I filed in the federal court
and I got a federal hearing.

00:35:15.750 --> 00:35:19.620
The federal court judge
stated that in the decision,

00:35:19.620 --> 00:35:24.002
he stated that if I presented
the State of Maryland

00:35:24.002 --> 00:35:26.160
with the last contention

00:35:26.160 --> 00:35:28.900
about the person I had found out had been,

00:35:28.900 --> 00:35:30.490
I had been wrongfully imprisoned for,

00:35:30.490 --> 00:35:35.490
if they didn't do anything
and I came back to their court

00:35:35.700 --> 00:35:38.560
they would grant me
the relief that I seek.

00:35:38.560 --> 00:35:42.010
So two years later, I get a letter

00:35:42.010 --> 00:35:44.260
from the Public Defender's Office saying

00:35:44.260 --> 00:35:49.260
that my hard work and
perseverance paid off.

00:35:51.280 --> 00:35:54.390
The photograph of the
man that I gave them,

00:35:54.390 --> 00:35:56.790
their investigators took it to the victim

00:35:56.790 --> 00:35:58.950
and he positively identified the man

00:35:58.950 --> 00:36:01.200
as being the person who robbed him,

00:36:01.200 --> 00:36:04.370
and he even had on the same shirt

00:36:04.370 --> 00:36:05.710
that he robbed him in.

00:36:05.710 --> 00:36:08.733
I mean, he had the same clothes on, right,

00:36:09.630 --> 00:36:12.100
when they committed the robbery he had on,

00:36:12.100 --> 00:36:14.720
so he positively identified him.

00:36:14.720 --> 00:36:17.690
The state then carried me back to court,

00:36:17.690 --> 00:36:22.350
they gave me a court release.

00:36:22.350 --> 00:36:26.170
They granted me a court release
from the 20-year sentence.

00:36:26.170 --> 00:36:31.170
And when I came home, I then
continue advocating for myself.

00:36:32.530 --> 00:36:35.670
I went back and I got a
full unconditional pardon

00:36:35.670 --> 00:36:37.670
from the Governor for
the State of Maryland,

00:36:37.670 --> 00:36:40.500
I became the first individual
who actually receive a pardon

00:36:40.500 --> 00:36:42.670
from the Governor unconditionally

00:36:42.670 --> 00:36:46.040
for the time that I'd
been wrongfully convicted.

00:36:46.040 --> 00:36:51.040
I then went forth and
filed for compensation

00:36:52.130 --> 00:36:56.950
up under a bill, a statute that
the state had at that time,

00:36:56.950 --> 00:37:00.790
and they awarded me $250,000

00:37:00.790 --> 00:37:04.096
for the 10 years I
spent wrongly in prison.

00:37:04.096 --> 00:37:06.350
I have four separate orders

00:37:06.350 --> 00:37:11.150
for expungements of the
criminal records of that case.

00:37:11.150 --> 00:37:14.300
But when I initially came home,

00:37:14.300 --> 00:37:15.970
the first month that I was home

00:37:15.970 --> 00:37:18.930
I spoke with a man by
the name of Al Sanders

00:37:18.930 --> 00:37:23.177
at WJZ-TV Channel 13 in Baltimore,

00:37:23.177 --> 00:37:27.100
and Mr. Sanders remembered
me and remembered my story

00:37:27.100 --> 00:37:29.410
as being a basketball player,

00:37:29.410 --> 00:37:32.480
he actually set up an interview

00:37:32.480 --> 00:37:37.020
for me to play a fellow
that was ranked number,

00:37:37.020 --> 00:37:39.150
I think he was ranked number one

00:37:39.150 --> 00:37:42.730
in the United States
as a basketball player,

00:37:42.730 --> 00:37:46.560
and they had me and him play one-on-one.

00:37:46.560 --> 00:37:48.420
I was at the time, I was 28,

00:37:48.420 --> 00:37:50.520
I think he might've been maybe 18.

00:37:50.520 --> 00:37:52.097
- [Mark] What was his name?

00:37:52.097 --> 00:37:53.297
- His name was Len Bias,

00:37:54.480 --> 00:37:56.960
and they filmed him and
I playing each other,

00:37:56.960 --> 00:37:59.360
and I beat him two games back-to-back.

00:37:59.360 --> 00:38:00.220
- [Mark] You beat him twice?

00:38:00.220 --> 00:38:01.053
- Twice.

00:38:01.053 --> 00:38:03.240
- Len Bias is a number two.
- That's my man.

00:38:03.240 --> 00:38:04.612
Yeah, he was number two,

00:38:04.612 --> 00:38:06.230
I think he was number two.
- Picked in the draft in '86?

00:38:06.230 --> 00:38:07.063
- Yeah.

00:38:09.207 --> 00:38:12.470
I didn't follow basketball
when I was locked up

00:38:12.470 --> 00:38:14.400
because it took too much out of me,

00:38:14.400 --> 00:38:17.610
it reminded me of all
that I was that I lost,

00:38:17.610 --> 00:38:19.610
that was taken from me.

00:38:19.610 --> 00:38:21.720
But I would take my frustration

00:38:21.720 --> 00:38:23.780
and anger out on the basketball court

00:38:23.780 --> 00:38:26.280
on every team that we played against.

00:38:26.280 --> 00:38:29.835
And we were beating
Virginia State University,

00:38:29.835 --> 00:38:33.270
Boise State University,

00:38:33.270 --> 00:38:35.613
Hagerstown Community College,

00:38:37.310 --> 00:38:40.460
BCCC, every institution or everybody,

00:38:40.460 --> 00:38:42.420
every team that they brought in there

00:38:42.420 --> 00:38:43.253
we were beating them,

00:38:43.253 --> 00:38:45.423
and we was beating them at will.

00:38:46.263 --> 00:38:47.096
- [Mark] This was a prison team?

00:38:47.096 --> 00:38:47.929
- In the prison team.

00:38:47.929 --> 00:38:49.786
But they wasn't writing that up though.

00:38:49.786 --> 00:38:52.310
(both chuckles)

00:38:52.310 --> 00:38:55.494
They didn't wanna broadcast
how we will beating them.

00:38:55.494 --> 00:38:56.327
- [Mark] You are six seven?

00:38:56.327 --> 00:38:58.272
- Yeah.
- Six foot seven.

00:38:58.272 --> 00:39:01.290
- Yeah, and I had an attitude

00:39:01.290 --> 00:39:04.220
that I don't care who thought you was,

00:39:04.220 --> 00:39:06.860
they had a guy, now I
forget, he was seven one,

00:39:06.860 --> 00:39:09.180
they brought him in there in Hagerstown

00:39:09.180 --> 00:39:11.570
and we played against them
and he was talking about

00:39:11.570 --> 00:39:13.190
how many points he was gonna score.

00:39:13.190 --> 00:39:16.090
I stopped him from scoring
any points, period.

00:39:16.090 --> 00:39:18.263
You don't get nothing, this is my house.

00:39:21.210 --> 00:39:24.080
After being coming home
and doing the things

00:39:24.080 --> 00:39:24.930
that I was doing,

00:39:25.880 --> 00:39:29.410
I felt like, I was in a process

00:39:29.410 --> 00:39:31.360
of trying to put my life back together.

00:39:32.250 --> 00:39:34.810
- [Mark] So your prime years
as a basketball player,

00:39:34.810 --> 00:39:37.513
18 to 28 were all spent in prison?

00:39:41.150 --> 00:39:43.180
- My children, I started having children,

00:39:43.180 --> 00:39:44.980
my first child was born in 1988.

00:39:48.090 --> 00:39:50.347
I started working, employed position.

00:39:50.347 --> 00:39:52.530
Initially when I would apply for jobs

00:39:52.530 --> 00:39:54.300
that criminal record would come up.

00:39:54.300 --> 00:39:57.210
Every time, even though
I had expungement orders

00:39:57.210 --> 00:39:59.360
a full unconditional pardon and all that,

00:39:59.360 --> 00:40:02.460
this criminal record was a burden.

00:40:02.460 --> 00:40:04.730
Every time I turned around it came up

00:40:04.730 --> 00:40:06.710
and I would contact the State of Maryland,

00:40:06.710 --> 00:40:09.260
the Governor, the
Controller, the Treasurer

00:40:09.260 --> 00:40:11.560
because these were the
people that I was directly

00:40:11.560 --> 00:40:15.430
involved in Maryland
regarding my conviction.

00:40:15.430 --> 00:40:17.110
And when I say that, I'm saying,

00:40:17.110 --> 00:40:19.690
I didn't talk to the assistant,

00:40:19.690 --> 00:40:21.990
I talked to the Governor.

00:40:21.990 --> 00:40:24.713
I mean, I went to these
people, they explained to them

00:40:24.713 --> 00:40:27.290
that this conviction was burdened me.

00:40:27.290 --> 00:40:29.600
I got a bachelor's degree in sociology

00:40:29.600 --> 00:40:31.500
from Towson State University.

00:40:31.500 --> 00:40:33.090
I was a certified paralegal.

00:40:33.090 --> 00:40:36.880
And then I also went back
to school and got 450 hours

00:40:36.880 --> 00:40:39.060
of computer office experience

00:40:39.060 --> 00:40:42.220
as a computer office specialist.

00:40:42.220 --> 00:40:44.370
But I couldn't get a job

00:40:45.385 --> 00:40:48.150
as a correctional officer,

00:40:48.150 --> 00:40:51.860
I couldn't get a job as a security guard.

00:40:51.860 --> 00:40:54.630
I tried to get a job, I left Maryland

00:40:54.630 --> 00:40:55.960
and went back to Brooklyn, New York,

00:40:55.960 --> 00:40:57.900
tried to get a job working there

00:40:57.900 --> 00:40:59.970
for a pharmaceutical company,

00:40:59.970 --> 00:41:02.640
and you had to be bonded because you dealt

00:41:02.640 --> 00:41:04.990
with different kinds
of medications that was

00:41:04.990 --> 00:41:07.540
considered controlled,
dangerous substances.

00:41:07.540 --> 00:41:09.550
When I applied for the position,

00:41:09.550 --> 00:41:11.370
they couldn't bond me because I had

00:41:11.370 --> 00:41:13.380
a criminal record for armed robbery.

00:41:13.380 --> 00:41:17.420
Even though I got all
these expungement orders

00:41:17.420 --> 00:41:22.280
and documentation, showing
that they had been destroyed,

00:41:22.280 --> 00:41:25.760
it was no longer accessible
and all these kinds of things.

00:41:25.760 --> 00:41:28.313
But those was just things
that I was being sent,

00:41:29.442 --> 00:41:31.092
and the conviction still came up.

00:41:33.483 --> 00:41:36.236
- [Mark] How long has this
remained on your record?

00:41:36.236 --> 00:41:40.140
- To show you how crazy it is, I was in,

00:41:40.140 --> 00:41:44.340
I left Maryland in 2018

00:41:44.340 --> 00:41:47.460
and went to Tampa, Florida
to work with an organization

00:41:47.460 --> 00:41:49.350
called The Sunny Center

00:41:49.350 --> 00:41:54.350
which is a organization
run by a former exoneree

00:41:56.180 --> 00:41:57.260
who was on death row,

00:41:57.260 --> 00:41:58.910
a lady by the name of Sunny Jacobs.

00:41:58.910 --> 00:42:03.910
Her husband actually was the
fellow who was electrocuted

00:42:04.280 --> 00:42:06.160
and his body caught on fire

00:42:06.160 --> 00:42:08.603
when they did the death
penalty against him.

00:42:10.090 --> 00:42:14.240
While there, I went through
things with them about working.

00:42:14.240 --> 00:42:18.080
So I left and I applied for a position

00:42:18.080 --> 00:42:22.753
in Oxnard, California
for a re-entry program,

00:42:24.181 --> 00:42:26.990
and that was done in
2020, which is this year.

00:42:26.990 --> 00:42:31.990
I drove from Maryland
to Oxnard, California.

00:42:33.970 --> 00:42:37.470
I reached there the
day after thanksgiving.

00:42:37.470 --> 00:42:40.980
I stayed there until February the 10th,

00:42:40.980 --> 00:42:45.980
when the live scanners or
background check came back

00:42:46.750 --> 00:42:49.560
and it had the criminal record on there.

00:42:49.560 --> 00:42:54.440
I contacted the State of
Maryland Treasurer's Department

00:42:54.440 --> 00:42:57.360
to notify them that
this has happened again.

00:42:57.360 --> 00:42:59.090
The last time that it happened

00:42:59.090 --> 00:43:03.830
they have paid me an
additional $50,000 in 1998.

00:43:03.830 --> 00:43:05.700
The Attorney General Joe Curran

00:43:05.700 --> 00:43:09.120
and them sent me a letter
telling me the date had expunged,

00:43:09.120 --> 00:43:12.100
they got a separate or
forth expungement order

00:43:13.521 --> 00:43:15.950
and that the record would
never come up ever again.

00:43:15.950 --> 00:43:18.450
I got a letter from the
administrative judge

00:43:18.450 --> 00:43:21.670
for the Circuit Court
saying that this conviction

00:43:21.670 --> 00:43:23.360
was removed from the record

00:43:23.360 --> 00:43:26.593
and we contacted the FBI Director,

00:43:27.997 --> 00:43:29.417
and I was like, "Wow, I ain't got to worry

00:43:29.417 --> 00:43:31.180
"about discipline no more,"

00:43:31.180 --> 00:43:33.353
here as I'm 62 years old,

00:43:34.816 --> 00:43:36.510
and I'm dealing with the
same thing that I was dealing

00:43:36.510 --> 00:43:38.333
with back when I was 17.

00:43:40.270 --> 00:43:43.850
Man, I lost everything, I
lost, my vehicle broke down

00:43:43.850 --> 00:43:47.963
and on my way from leaving
California, when I was told

00:43:47.963 --> 00:43:51.523
that this conviction came
up again I lost everything

00:43:51.523 --> 00:43:54.600
that I owned, everything.

00:43:54.600 --> 00:43:57.463
And it's like I'm starting
over at the age of 62.

00:43:59.043 --> 00:44:01.360
- [Mark] What was your
lowest point in all of this?

00:44:01.360 --> 00:44:04.590
- The lowest point
really was just recently,

00:44:04.590 --> 00:44:07.250
the other day when we was talking.

00:44:07.250 --> 00:44:10.330
I got a letter, I hadn't
checked my emails and stuff,

00:44:10.330 --> 00:44:13.131
but I had contacted the State of Maryland,

00:44:13.131 --> 00:44:14.950
I was trying to make
an agreement with them

00:44:14.950 --> 00:44:17.680
so that they could settle this
claim that I have with them,

00:44:17.680 --> 00:44:20.830
so that I could actually try
to get get a place to live

00:44:20.830 --> 00:44:23.483
and get some of my stuff back and all.

00:44:24.760 --> 00:44:29.460
I sent a letter that the agent,

00:44:29.460 --> 00:44:33.290
the state agent sent me,
in the letter he's asked,

00:44:33.290 --> 00:44:36.080
he's telling me that the conviction

00:44:36.080 --> 00:44:38.290
that I'm talking about
that's on the record

00:44:39.280 --> 00:44:42.453
had nothing to do with my
wrongful imprisonment case.

00:44:43.440 --> 00:44:46.287
And I'm looking at it
and I'm saying to myself,

00:44:46.287 --> 00:44:49.147
"I know these people was not
even start doing this man."

00:44:50.080 --> 00:44:53.637
I didn't have no criminal
record when I went to prison,

00:44:53.637 --> 00:44:57.900
the Governor gave me a full-on
conditional pardon dating

00:44:57.900 --> 00:45:02.650
from my arrest in 1975,
which is February 15th, 1975

00:45:03.730 --> 00:45:08.730
until I was released August of 1986.

00:45:09.140 --> 00:45:12.380
So anything that happened
between that time

00:45:12.380 --> 00:45:16.980
of me being incarcerated is
supposed to have been destroyed

00:45:16.980 --> 00:45:18.670
because that's what expungement means.

00:45:18.670 --> 00:45:20.260
After three years from the date

00:45:20.260 --> 00:45:22.680
of the issuance of the
order from the court

00:45:22.680 --> 00:45:26.220
they supposed to destroy
all reference to that case.

00:45:26.220 --> 00:45:27.793
This is 2020,

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:33.350
what am I counting wrong
or something? (chuckles)

00:45:33.350 --> 00:45:35.980
I meant, not trying to
be funny, but honestly.

00:45:35.980 --> 00:45:38.440
And to, you know, the
thing about it is that

00:45:38.440 --> 00:45:42.840
I'm living, right now,
temporarily in quarters

00:45:42.840 --> 00:45:46.800
with my nephew teenager, a kid.

00:45:46.800 --> 00:45:48.280
I mean, I was a teenager.

00:45:48.280 --> 00:45:49.930
I remember when I was a teenager.

00:45:51.460 --> 00:45:52.950
I was set in my ways.

00:45:52.950 --> 00:45:54.210
I had my own home,

00:45:54.210 --> 00:45:56.460
I had my own business going,

00:45:56.460 --> 00:45:58.880
I worked for the child support enforcement

00:45:58.880 --> 00:45:59.960
as a fiscal auditor.

00:45:59.960 --> 00:46:01.330
I worked for the State of Maryland

00:46:01.330 --> 00:46:03.233
as employment placement specialist.

00:46:04.230 --> 00:46:06.010
I worked for the Public Defender's Office

00:46:06.010 --> 00:46:09.130
as a paralegal intake specialist.

00:46:09.130 --> 00:46:11.380
All my documentation
showed that my positions

00:46:11.380 --> 00:46:14.450
I worked at all of substance, you know.

00:46:14.450 --> 00:46:17.580
And now you're gonna treat me as though

00:46:17.580 --> 00:46:19.710
I have no understanding of what's going on

00:46:19.710 --> 00:46:21.870
because you don't wanna recognize the fact

00:46:21.870 --> 00:46:25.410
that I know what I'm doing as a paralegal.

00:46:25.410 --> 00:46:29.120
But mind you, it still
doesn't change the fact,

00:46:29.120 --> 00:46:30.760
I was wrongfully imprisoned.

00:46:30.760 --> 00:46:34.340
I wasn't one that committed the crime,

00:46:34.340 --> 00:46:36.180
so why should I continue to live

00:46:36.180 --> 00:46:38.460
and bear the burden of what was actually

00:46:39.830 --> 00:46:42.383
a criminal record that
should never have been.

00:46:43.717 --> 00:46:45.870
And then you tell people
that it's been expunged

00:46:45.870 --> 00:46:47.390
and you can try to give the impression

00:46:47.390 --> 00:46:50.900
that you did what you did to
assist me and you never did.

00:46:50.900 --> 00:46:53.833
All you did was do things
that was hindrance to me.

00:46:55.140 --> 00:46:57.410
The part that hurt the
most of all about this

00:46:57.410 --> 00:46:59.530
is that in 2004, while working for the

00:46:59.530 --> 00:47:01.750
Department of Labor,
Licensing and Regulation

00:47:01.750 --> 00:47:04.430
I had custody of four children.

00:47:04.430 --> 00:47:07.960
I was working, I was part
owner to a bail bond business

00:47:07.960 --> 00:47:12.510
at that time, and was accused
of a crime that I knew nothing

00:47:12.510 --> 00:47:15.083
about just like the same
thing with that first one.

00:47:16.684 --> 00:47:18.370
And they held me without bail

00:47:18.370 --> 00:47:19.770
based on the same criminal records

00:47:19.770 --> 00:47:24.770
that the state in 1998
gave me $50,000 for.

00:47:25.290 --> 00:47:28.650
And I purchased the
transcript of the trial

00:47:29.610 --> 00:47:33.120
because I couldn't believe
what was being done.

00:47:33.120 --> 00:47:35.410
And it was being done blatantly

00:47:35.410 --> 00:47:39.230
with disregard for my rights whatsoever.

00:47:39.230 --> 00:47:43.150
It was something that I go
through, I really go through.

00:47:43.150 --> 00:47:44.890
- [Mark] So what was the second?

00:47:44.890 --> 00:47:47.070
- I was found not guilty of everything,

00:47:47.070 --> 00:47:50.403
but my kids was, my daughter,

00:47:52.258 --> 00:47:54.780
Alicia was 12,

00:47:54.780 --> 00:47:57.510
Andrea was 11,

00:47:57.510 --> 00:48:00.290
my son was Diamante was seven,

00:48:00.290 --> 00:48:03.420
and my youngest daughter Aleija was four.

00:48:03.420 --> 00:48:05.540
And they put them in foster care,

00:48:05.540 --> 00:48:07.070
and they held them in foster care

00:48:07.070 --> 00:48:08.410
for that whole period of time

00:48:08.410 --> 00:48:10.928
while I was confined,
waiting to go to trial.

00:48:10.928 --> 00:48:11.761
- [Mark] What were you accused of?

00:48:11.761 --> 00:48:15.740
- Because I was accused of
being involved in a stabbing

00:48:15.740 --> 00:48:19.230
that I had nothing to do
with and no knowledge of.

00:48:19.230 --> 00:48:22.450
I went to trial for three days.

00:48:22.450 --> 00:48:26.740
The judge that they actually got out,

00:48:26.740 --> 00:48:29.610
he was retired, he had retired,
they went in and got him

00:48:29.610 --> 00:48:30.530
out of retirement.

00:48:30.530 --> 00:48:33.550
His name was Judge Roger
Brown in Baltimore.

00:48:33.550 --> 00:48:37.853
They got him out of retirement
for him to handle my case.

00:48:38.990 --> 00:48:40.830
And one of the bench officers,

00:48:40.830 --> 00:48:44.217
Judge Brown says to state prosecutor,

00:48:44.217 --> 00:48:47.523
"This is the kind of shit
y'all put this man through?

00:48:48.387 --> 00:48:50.950
"He should sue, you all again."

00:48:50.950 --> 00:48:52.160
And I'm saying,

00:48:53.406 --> 00:48:57.590
this is a certified copy
of the trial transcript.

00:48:57.590 --> 00:49:00.510
I'm not saying paper,
I'm talking about video.

00:49:00.510 --> 00:49:03.810
You can see us standing
at there and him talking,

00:49:03.810 --> 00:49:05.607
telling the prosecutor that,

00:49:05.607 --> 00:49:08.500
"Mr. Vass should sue your ass again."

00:49:08.500 --> 00:49:10.670
That's how he said it.

00:49:10.670 --> 00:49:12.640
And when I look at that

00:49:12.640 --> 00:49:15.077
I think back to it, I said to myself,

00:49:15.077 --> 00:49:17.927
"They don't realize the
damage that was done

00:49:17.927 --> 00:49:20.667
"when my children, you subject my kids

00:49:20.667 --> 00:49:22.730
"to this kind of stuff."

00:49:22.730 --> 00:49:26.490
Do they understand that,
right now, my daughter and I,

00:49:27.726 --> 00:49:30.350
not Alicia but my other
daughter Andrea and I

00:49:30.350 --> 00:49:32.100
are estranged behind that.

00:49:32.100 --> 00:49:34.170
Because whatever happened during the time

00:49:34.170 --> 00:49:37.150
of her being in foster care

00:49:37.150 --> 00:49:38.703
she don't wanna talk about it.

00:49:40.140 --> 00:49:43.080
In a way, it's like, I
feel like I'm at fault

00:49:43.080 --> 00:49:45.180
for it because of what happened.

00:49:45.180 --> 00:49:50.180
And then, you know, I've
try to not allow myself

00:49:50.310 --> 00:49:51.780
to stress about it,

00:49:51.780 --> 00:49:53.480
but I have to 'cause I'm a parent.

00:49:54.940 --> 00:49:59.940
I have grandchildren now that
I focus my life be about.

00:50:00.200 --> 00:50:02.070
This that I'm dealing with now

00:50:02.070 --> 00:50:05.090
is really hard because it's like

00:50:05.090 --> 00:50:06.940
I've worked all the years that I did

00:50:06.940 --> 00:50:10.810
to try to reintegrate
myself back in to society

00:50:10.810 --> 00:50:12.970
as a positive person.

00:50:12.970 --> 00:50:14.860
I've worked with different organizations,

00:50:14.860 --> 00:50:16.573
have documentation to show it,

00:50:18.259 --> 00:50:22.750
and still the State of Maryland
do not own up to the truth.

00:50:22.750 --> 00:50:25.783
You messed up, you messed up,
not once, but several times.

00:50:26.720 --> 00:50:31.410
But that mess up has been
devastating to my life,

00:50:31.410 --> 00:50:34.033
and I feel that they should
do what they supposed to do.

00:50:35.420 --> 00:50:36.643
- [Mark] Were there
moments where you just felt

00:50:36.643 --> 00:50:39.235
like this was a dream?

00:50:39.235 --> 00:50:40.535
- (chuckles) Yeah, really.

00:50:41.818 --> 00:50:43.180
A lot of times I thought,
I couldn't believe

00:50:43.180 --> 00:50:45.883
that this was going on, and
that it's still going on.

00:50:48.240 --> 00:50:49.270
- [Mark] Did they ever catch the person

00:50:49.270 --> 00:50:50.780
that did the robbery?

00:50:50.780 --> 00:50:52.110
- Yeah, yeah.

00:50:52.110 --> 00:50:54.230
Well, they were not saying
they had to catch him,

00:50:54.230 --> 00:50:56.180
they already knew who he was.

00:50:56.180 --> 00:50:57.910
They knew who he was.
- He was the informer.

00:50:57.910 --> 00:51:00.937
- There was nothing that
they could do to him

00:51:00.937 --> 00:51:02.170
because what happened is,

00:51:02.170 --> 00:51:05.223
the statute of limitation was 10 years.

00:51:06.479 --> 00:51:09.910
Only time that a person can
be charged with anything

00:51:09.910 --> 00:51:12.960
is if it was a charge that
was carrying a life sentence,

00:51:12.960 --> 00:51:16.362
if it was a rape or murder, right.

00:51:16.362 --> 00:51:18.140
They carry life sentences

00:51:18.140 --> 00:51:20.890
so there's no statute
of limitation on that.

00:51:20.890 --> 00:51:23.570
But I try to, you know,
I would like to ask

00:51:23.570 --> 00:51:25.557
Governor Hogan in Maryland,

00:51:25.557 --> 00:51:26.957
"What is the statute of limitation

00:51:26.957 --> 00:51:28.487
"when you do the things that you do

00:51:28.487 --> 00:51:32.277
"to deprive your
constituents of the freedom

00:51:32.277 --> 00:51:35.203
"of their life, their livelihood,

00:51:37.830 --> 00:51:39.067
"and when the evidence
is presented to you,

00:51:39.067 --> 00:51:41.707
"instead you acknowledged
that something has been wrong

00:51:41.707 --> 00:51:45.487
"and done wrong, admit it and correct it

00:51:45.487 --> 00:51:47.940
"or do whatever you can to correct it."

00:51:47.940 --> 00:51:50.150
But that's not how they look at this.

00:51:50.150 --> 00:51:53.880
I feel like people don't
understand the gravity

00:51:53.880 --> 00:51:56.190
of the things that go on behind this.

00:51:56.190 --> 00:51:57.023
It's a lot.

00:51:57.906 --> 00:52:00.770
You don't have nothing but
time to stop and think.

00:52:00.770 --> 00:52:01.880
I try to stay on my form,

00:52:01.880 --> 00:52:04.050
I try to stay on the ball (laughs),

00:52:04.050 --> 00:52:06.160
I try to do everything that I can

00:52:06.160 --> 00:52:08.750
to not allow me to sit there and reflect

00:52:08.750 --> 00:52:10.950
over the things that have been going on.

00:52:10.950 --> 00:52:12.700
The things that have been done,

00:52:12.700 --> 00:52:15.430
and the things that I've been deprived of

00:52:15.430 --> 00:52:17.370
having the opportunity to network

00:52:17.370 --> 00:52:20.420
with my children and my
grandkids and stuff like that

00:52:20.420 --> 00:52:23.340
because of the circumstances
that I've been dealing with.

00:52:23.340 --> 00:52:26.460
And the thing of about it
is that I started out at 17

00:52:26.460 --> 00:52:28.850
and I'm 62 now, you know.

00:52:30.847 --> 00:52:31.680
- [Mark] Leslie.

00:52:31.680 --> 00:52:32.850
Well, thank you so much
for sharing your story.

00:52:32.850 --> 00:52:33.683
- Thank you Mark.

00:52:33.683 --> 00:52:35.030
- [Mark] It's an incredible story.

00:52:35.030 --> 00:52:36.217
- Thank you, Mark.

